wpa_supplicant and Wi-Fi Aware Public Key Bootstrapping (PKBST)
==================================================================

This document describes how the NAN Public Key Bootstrapping (PKBST)
and pairwise identity caching of Wi-Fi Aware R5 are configured and used
through the wpa_supplicant control interface.

With PKBST, NAN devices pair without user interaction by using Public
Key Records (PKRs) provisioned in advance, e.g., from a companion app.
A PKR carries an Individual Public Key (IPK, compressed P-256), an
Individual Identity Key (IIK), optionally Group Identity Keys (GIKs),
a Valid Duration, and a mandatory Record Signature made with the
Individual Secret Key (ISK) that belongs to the IPK.

In one-way PKBST only the publisher's record is used; in mutual PKBST
both devices' records are used. The SAE password for pairing is
derived from the IPK(s), and each device that owns a used record proves
possession of its ISK with a FILS Key Confirmation element in PASN.


Record format
-------------

Records are exchanged as hex strings holding the body of a Public Key
Record attribute with a single record:

  Number of PKRs (1) | Length | Version | Record Control |
  Cipher Version | IPK | IIK | Valid Duration | [optional fields] |
  Record Signature

All record fields are big-endian. Records that are unsigned, carry an
invalid signature, or use an unsupported version are rejected.


Record provisioning
-------------------

NAN_SET self_pkr <hex>
	Install the local record. The previous record stays in place if
	the new one is invalid. Installing a record with a different IPK
	clears the configured ISK.

NAN_SET self_isk <hex>
	Set the 32-octet ISK of the local record. The ISK is never exported.

NAN_SET generate_self_pkr 1
	Generate a new local key pair, IIK, and record. The Operation
	Control is set to one-time use and the Valid Duration is set to five
	minutes. The GIKs of the current record are kept; the first generated
	record has one random GIK.

NAN_GET self_pkr
	Export the local record, signed with the ISK, for provisioning on
	peers. Fails if no ISK matching the record is configured.

NAN_SET peer_pkr [<peer NMI> ]<hex>
	Install a peer record. Without a peer address, the record is staged
	and applied to the next discovered peer.

The IIK and GIKs of the local record, and the IIK of peer records, are
used for NAN Tag generation and resolution in the IRSA.


Discovery, bootstrapping and pairing
------------------------------------

NAN_SET extended_pkbst_method <0-3>
	Supported PKBST methods: bit 0 one-way, bit 1 mutual.

Services advertise PKBST with the extended_pbm=<value> parameter of
NAN_PUBLISH and NAN_SUBSCRIBE. The method used with a peer is one that
both devices support, preferring mutual PKBST.

PKBST bootstrap follow-up frames carry the selected one-way or mutual
method in the PBEA Extended Pairing Bootstrapping Method field and use
an NPBA Pairing Bootstrapping Method value of zero. PKBST is accepted
only when the advertised methods are mutually supported and the
required Public Key Records are provisioned.

NAN_BOOTSTRAP ... method=<1|2>
	With PKBST configured, method 1 requests one-way and method 2
	requests mutual PKBST bootstrapping.

NAN_PAIR ... auth=1
	When one-way or mutual PKBST was negotiated with the peer, the SAE
	password is derived from the records and any configured password is
	ignored.
	Pairing fails if a needed record is missing, expired, or revoked, or
	if the peer's identity confirmation is missing or invalid.

One-time-use records are currently not deleted automatically after
pairing setup.


Pairwise identity caching
-------------------------

NAN_SET pik_caching 1
	Enable NPK/PIK caching. When both devices support it, they derive a
	Pairwise Identity Key (PIK) after pairing setup instead of
	exchanging NIKs.

The PIK is cached with the NPK for each paired peer, is kept across NAN
restarts, and is freed when the interface is removed. The device then
includes Pairing NAN Tags (PNTags) derived from the cached PIKs in its
SDFs, resolves received PNTags with them, and uses them for pairing
verification (NAN_PAIR ... auth=2).


Record updates
--------------

NAN_UPDATE_PKR [peer_nmi=<addr>] [generate=1] [op_ctrl=<value>]
		       [send=1] [send_revoked=1]
	Update the local record and optionally send it to a paired peer in a
	Protected Transport frame. generate=1 creates a new local record.
	op_ctrl sets the Operation Control field of the local record. send=1
	sends the local record; with send_revoked=1, the record replaced by
	the last generate=1 is also sent as revoked, asking the peer to delete
	it. Without peer_nmi, the first paired peer is used.

Received updates are accepted only from paired peers and only in
protected frames. Each accepted change is reported with:

  NAN-PKR-UPDATE addr=<peer NMI> iik=<IIK> revoked=<0|1>


Limitations
-----------

- Valid After, Valid Before, and Device and Service Info of received
  records are parsed but not used; validity is based on Valid Duration.
